# E0600: constant folding is the same wherever it is written. A constant
# integer operation that overflows its type, and a division by a constant zero,
# have no value at run time either -- C wraps the one (`-fwrapv`) and traps on
# the other -- so a body refuses them as a unit-level definition does
# (const_overflow.z). Each of these compiled: `BIG + BIG` and `BIG * 2` reached
# C as a wrap, the narrow forms wrapped in their width, and `1 / 0` trapped.
#
# An operand that is a variable makes the operation a run-time one, which still
# wraps in its width (examples/narrow_widths.z). A u64 operation the i64
# evaluator cannot hold is not an overflow: `9223372036854775807.u64 + 1.u64`
# fits its type, runs, and is not reported here.
BIG: 9223372036854775807

main: function is {
    n: 3
    if (BIG + BIG) > n then print "sum above n"
    if (BIG * 2) > n then print "product above n"
    print "\{9223372036854775807 + 1}"
    print "\{200.u8 * 2.u8} \{127.i8 + 1.i8}"
    print "\{1 / 0}"
    print "\{9223372036854775807.u64 + 1.u64}"
}
